---
import '../styles/tokens.css'
import '../styles/landing.css'

const TOOLS = [
  ['get_scratchpad', 'read', 'Every line the learner has written, each line’s verdict, and which one broke first.'],
  ['check_work', 'write', 'Runs the page’s computer algebra system over the whole derivation and writes the badges.'],
  ['annotate_step', 'write', 'Puts an explanation in the margin beside one specific line.'],
  ['propose_step', 'write', 'Offers a replacement line. The learner accepts or rejects it; the agent cannot apply it.'],
  ['new_problem', 'write', 'Generates a fresh problem in the same family, answer derived here rather than stored.'],
  ['get_receipt', 'read', 'What the session observed, attributed, including what it does not establish.'],
]
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <link
      rel="icon"
      href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 32 32'%3E%3Crect width='32' height='32' rx='6' fill='%2316150F'/%3E%3Cpath d='M9 22V10l7 8 7-8v12' stroke='%23FAFAF7' stroke-width='2.4' fill='none' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-linecap='round'/%3E%3C/svg%3E"
    />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Second Try by Mathos. The page checks the learner's working step by step, and WebMCP lets an agent read it, explain it, and offer a fix the learner decides about."
    />
    <title>Second Try — Mathos</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

    <header class="nav">
      <a class="wordmark" href="/">Mathos</a>
      <p class="nav-mid">Second Try</p>
      <a class="nav-cta" href="/learn">Open the scratchpad <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </header>

    <main id="main" class="container">
      <section class="hero">
        <p class="kicker">WebMCP Challenge · Mathos</p>
        <hr class="rule" />
        <div class="hero-split">
          <div class="hero-text">
            <h1 class="hero-line">
              The agent cannot tell you your&nbsp;work is right.
              <span class="hero-dim">Only the page can.</span>
            </h1>
            <p class="hero-lede">
              A learner writes their working, line by line, on a page that holds a real computer
              algebra system. WebMCP hands an agent tools to read that working, ask the page to check
              it, explain the line that broke, and offer a replacement the learner has to accept.
              The verdict is written by the engine, never by the model.
            </p>
            <a class="button button-lg" href="/learn"><span>Open the scratchpad</span><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </div>

          <div class="demo-strip" aria-label="What the page does">
        <div class="strip-line strip-sound">
          <span class="strip-n">1</span>
          <code>12x^3 + 4x^2</code>
          <span class="strip-badge sound">follows</span>
        </div>
        <div class="strip-line strip-sound">
          <span class="strip-n">2</span>
          <code>36x^2 + 8x</code>
          <span class="strip-badge sound">differentiates</span>
        </div>
        <div class="strip-line strip-broken">
          <span class="strip-n">3</span>
          <code>36x^2</code>
          <span class="strip-badge broken">not equivalent</span>
          <p class="strip-note">Short of the line above by 8x</p>
        </div>
        <div class="strip-line strip-after">
          <span class="strip-n">4</span>
          <code>144</code>
          <span class="strip-badge after">after the first break</span>
        </div>
          <p class="strip-caption">
            Not &ldquo;your answer is wrong&rdquo;. The first line that stopped being true, and what
            is missing from it. Lines after the break are dimmed, not accused.
          </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="why">
        <p class="kicker">Why this needs the browser</p>
        <hr class="rule" />
        <div class="why-grid">
          <div class="why-item">
            <h2>The work has never been sent anywhere</h2>
            <p>
              A half-finished derivation, mid-edit, is not in any database. A backend MCP server
              cannot return what was never submitted to it. The agent reads it because the page
              chooses to expose it.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="why-item">
            <h2>The page can do what the model cannot</h2>
            <p>
              Symbolic equivalence checking is a canonical language-model failure. The page lends
              the agent a capability it provably lacks, and keeps authorship of the answer.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="why-item">
            <h2>Two writers share one document</h2>
            <p>
              A person is editing while the agent acts. That is the only reason a revision number
              means anything here — a stale write is rejected because the learner moved first.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="proof">
        <p class="kicker">The part worth testing</p>
        <hr class="rule" />
        <h2 class="proof-line">Tell the agent to say a broken line is correct.</h2>
        <p class="proof-lede">
          It will say so. The badge still reads <em>not equivalent</em>, because the badge is
          rendered from the engine&rsquo;s return value and the agent never touches it. An agent can
          be wrong about mathematics on this page without being able to make the page wrong.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="tools">
        <p class="kicker">Six tools · two read, four write</p>
        <hr class="rule" />
        <ul class="tool-list">
          {TOOLS.map(([name, kind, blurb]) => (
            <li>
              <code>{name}</code>
              <span class={`tool-kind kind-${kind}`}>{kind}</span>
              <p>{blurb}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="tools-note">
          Registered statically, once. Gating is a returned refusal with a recovery string, never an
          unregistration — a tool that disappears takes the reason with it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="honest">
        <p class="kicker">What it does not claim</p>
        <hr class="rule" />
        <p class="honest-body">
          Passing here is evidence about one session in one browser. It is not proof of
          understanding, and the page says so on the evidence surface itself. The supported
          mathematics is bounded: derivatives of polynomial expressions and algebraic rewriting.
          When the two verification routes disagree, the page reports that it could not determine
          the answer rather than guessing.
        </p>
        <a class="button" href="/learn"><span>Open the scratchpad</span><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="foot">
      <p>Mathos · Y Combinator W24</p>
      <p>Built for the WebMCP Challenge, 2026.</p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
